{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/orch-lane/*roundA*findings.md; rg -n 'execution-budget|hooks/program|safepoint/program|run-main|include-unit' src/cljc/yang/php/antlr* test/yang/php/antlr/harness*; nl -ba src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/safepoint.cljc; rg -n 'fesc|shutdown|budget|finally' collab/orch-lane/*retro* collab/orch-lane/*architect* 2>/dev/null\"","aggregated_output":"Created-Local: 2026-11-05 0x:xx +0700\nCoding-Agent: muse (muse-spark-1.3-contributor, laptop)\nWorktree: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-p1fix (branch feat/p1-forward-fixes @ origin/master)\nReview-Basis: collab/orch-lane/1794580000001-reviewer-retro-step7.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\nCommit: 0eade311 fix(yang.php): the P1 forward fixes — exhaustion escape, exit semantics, buffer drain, budget validation (no push)\n\n# Findings: Round A — the P1 forward fixes (owner-authorized)\n\nAll five review-named items are implemented, each with its regression. No P2\nscope was touched (request wiring, $_REQUEST merge, header/cookie/error-trigger,\nP9 surfaces stay for Round B). No fixture pins or oracle text were changed; new\nfixtures carry :oracle :pending via the `fx` default.\n\n## 1. P7-a exhaustion escape (P1) — DONE\n\n- `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc`: new `php/abandon-to` (pop frames\n  WITHOUT running :finally thunks) and `php/call-terminal` (call-ec shaped,\n  escape via abandon-to). New `php/root-fatal`: invokes the unit-root fatal\n  escape with `{:php/terminal :fatal, :php/operational true, :message}`.\n- `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/safepoint.cljc`: `php.sp/exhaust!` now calls\n  `php/root-fatal`; the old `unwind-to` + `raise` sequence (which executed\n  guest finally blocks and let `return` cancel the fatal) is gone. Fallback to\n  the old sequence only when no root escape is installed (exhaustion outside\n  `run-main`, which the harness never produces).\n- Regression: `safepoint-test/exhaustion-inside-try-finally-is-fatal-test` —\n  `try { while(true){} } finally { echo 'F'; }` under budget 4 terminates with\n  exactly the pinned fatal text; `F` is never printed.\n\n## 2. P7-a shutdown path (P1) — DONE\n\n- `php/run-main` reworked around three nested terminal escapes: a fatal escape\n  covering body + handler + shutdown, an exit escape covering the body phase,\n  and a shutdown escape covering the shutdown phase.\n- Operational failure in the shutdown `php/try` (`exc2`) now terminates with\n  `:php/fatal` instead of flowing through `snapshot-exc` as a guest Throwable.\n  Exception-handler-phase (`handle-uncaught`) exhaustion likewise routes to the\n  fatal escape.\n- Regression: `safepoint-test/exhaustion-in-shutdown-is-fatal-test` — a\n  shutdown callback that exhausts the budget yields `A` + fatal line, with no\n  catchable Throwable surfacing.\n\n## 3. P7-a budget validation (P2) — DONE\n\n- `safepoint/program` (host-side Clojure) rejects anything outside the\n  non-negative integer domain with `ex-info`\n  `{:yang.php.antlr.safepoint/error :invalid-budget}` — a named host-side\n  error, not a guest diagnostic.\n- Regression: `safepoint-test/budget-validation-test` covers -1, 1.5, \"4\".\n\n## 4. P5 exit semantics (P1) — DONE\n\n- `php/exit` now goes through `php/root-exit` (records status, invokes the\n  root exit continuation) instead of `php/raise`: `finally` blocks are skipped\n  and a return-from-finally cannot cancel termination. Exit during shutdown\n  records its status and aborts the shutdown sequence via the shutdown escape.\n  The old `:php/type :exit` raise path is retained only as a fallback when no\n  root escape is installed. Shutdown functions and destructors still run on the\n  normal exit path; status is preserved through shutdown (latest status wins).\n- Regressions (new `programs-p5` fixtures, :oracle :pending):\n  `:exit-skips-finally` (`try { exit(7); } finally { echo 'F'; }` → out \"\", status 7);\n  `:exit-in-finally-wins` (`try { echo 'A'; } finally { exit(3); }` → out \"A\", status 3).\n\n## 5. P5 unclosed buffers (P1) — DONE\n\n- `php/shutdown!` calls new `php/drain-buffers!` after the destructor phase and\n  before releasing globals: while more than one buffer is open, it reuses the\n  proven `php/bi-ob-end-flush` path, folding nested buffers innermost-first\n  into buffer zero. Destructor output buffered during the destructor phase is\n  included. (An earlier draft folded chunks with a novel str-join loop and\n  broke on guest chunk representation; the ob_end_flush reuse was verified\n  chunk-order-correct: nested `a`/`b` drains as `ab`.)\n- Regression: `:unclosed-buffer-drains` (`ob_start(); echo 'x';` → out \"x\").\n\n## Gate counts (this session, observed)\n\n- 20 tests / 178 assertions / 0 failures, 0 errors across\n  safepoint-test, request-context-test (incl. the 3 new P5 fixtures),\n  exceptions-test, finalization-test, generators-test.\n- prelude-test / include-test / builtin-surfaces-test: 4 failures + 4 errors\n  IDENTICAL on the unmodified tree (verified via stash baseline) — pre-existing\n  and unrelated (linked-profile/module-spec environment issues, one\n  array-path-assign case). Untouched by this change.\n- clj-kondo / cljstyle: no repo-configured gate found for these paths\n  (only a `:kondo` alias exists; no kondo config dir or CI lint step located\n  for the touched namespaces) — not run; the focused suites above are the gate.\n- No e2e / slow / subsystem suites per the laptop rule.\n\n## Escalations\n\n- None blocking. One note for the seat: the working tree was committed during\n  this session as 0eade311 with exactly the task-specified message (I did not\n  push). A sibling r2 findings file\n  (`1794630000000-engineer-roundA-r2...`) appeared in collab/orch-lane from\n  another seat process — no content conflict with this report.\n- No design decision needed round-tripping: the root escapes live entirely\n  inside the `run-main` unit boundary, so the include-unit-boundary question\n  the task flagged did not arise.\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-05\nCoding-Agent: muse (muse-spark, laptop)\nTask: Round A r2 — finish the P1 forward fixes (resume-by-state)\n\n## Per-item state\n\nAll five items were found already implemented in WIP commit 2aae3395.\nI made no source changes; I verified, gated, committed, and recorded.\n\n1. P7-a exhaustion escape (P1) — FOUND COMPLETE. safepoint exhaust!\n   routes through php/root-fatal (terminal root escape via\n   php/call-terminal + php/abandon-to, skipping :finally thunks).\n   Regression: exhaustion-inside-try-finally-is-fatal-test.\n2. P7-a shutdown path (P1) — FOUND COMPLETE. run-main recognizes\n   operational exc2 from the shutdown try and re-escapes through the\n   fatal continuation instead of snapshot-exc. Regression:\n   exhaustion-in-shutdown-is-fatal-test.\n3. P7-a budget validation (P2) — FOUND COMPLETE. safepoint/program\n   rejects non-non-negative-integers with a named host-side ex-info\n   (:invalid-budget). Regression: budget-validation-test.\n4. P5 exit semantics (P1) — FOUND COMPLETE. php/exit goes through\n   php/root-exit (distinct root continuation, status preserved via\n   php.rt/exit-status through shutdown). Regressions:\n   :exit-skips-finally, :exit-in-finally-wins.\n5. P5 unclosed buffers (P1) — FOUND COMPLETE. php/drain-buffers!\n   folds nested buffers via ob_end_flush after the destructor phase,\n   before releasing globals. Regression: :unclosed-buffer-drains.\n\nNew fixtures carry :oracle :pending via the fx helper default; no\nfixture pins or oracle text were changed. No P2 scope touched.\n\n## Gates (laptop-legal)\n\n- clojure -M:test -n yang.php.antlr.safepoint-test: 5 tests,\n  20 assertions, 0 failures.\n- clojure -M:test -n yang.php.antlr.request-context-test\n  -n yang.php.antlr.builtin-surfaces-test: 5 tests, 142 assertions,\n  0 failures.\n- clj -M:kondo --lint on safepoint.cljc + prelude.cljc: 0 errors,\n  0 warnings.\n- cljstyle: not runnable on this laptop (no cljstyle alias/binary);\n  not run. No e2e/slow/subsystem suites run (laptop rule).\n\n## Escalations\n\nNone. No design decision needed round-tripping through the seat.\nzsh:1: no matches found: test/yang/php/antlr/harness*\n     1\t(ns yang.php.antlr.safepoint\n     2\t  \"The PHP `:loop` and `:call` hooks. A bounded run charges one safepoint to\n     3\t   its per-run counter; exhaustion unwinds past guest handlers and raises the\n     4\t   operational fatal, not a guest Throwable.\"\n     5\t  (:require\n     6\t    [yang.core.uast :as u]))\n     7\t\n     8\t\n     9\t(def profile\n    10\t  {:loop 'php.sp/loop, :call {:hook 'php.sp/call, :sequence true}})\n    11\t\n    12\t\n    13\t(def fatal-text\n    14\t  \"Maximum execution time of 30 seconds exceeded\")\n    15\t\n    16\t\n    17\t(def ^:private function-definitions\n    18\t  '[[php.sp/exhaust!\n    19\t     ;; the terminal root escape: guest handlers, returns and finalizers\n    20\t     ;; cannot replace the operational fatal\n    21\t     (fn []\n    22\t       (php/root-fatal \"Maximum execution time of 30 seconds exceeded\"))]\n    23\t    [php.sp/tick\n    24\t     (fn []\n    25\t       (let [n (cell/get php.sp/remaining)]\n    26\t         (if (= n 0)\n    27\t           (php.sp/exhaust!)\n    28\t           (do (cell/set! php.sp/remaining (- n 1)) :php/null))))]\n    29\t    [php.sp/loop (fn [] (php.sp/tick))]\n    30\t    [php.sp/call (fn [] (php.sp/tick))]])\n    31\t\n    32\t\n    33\t(defn- definitions->uast\n    34\t  [defs]\n    35\t  (u/seq-nodes (map (fn [[k form]] (u/def! k (u/sexp->uast form))) defs)))\n    36\t\n    37\t\n    38\t(defn program\n    39\t  \"The bounded run: hook definitions and a counter holding `budget`, then the\n    40\t   safepoint-derived program `ast`. `budget` must be a non-negative integer;\n    41\t   anything else is a named host-side error, not a guest diagnostic.\"\n    42\t  [budget ast]\n    43\t  (when-not (and (integer? budget) (not (neg? budget)))\n    44\t    (throw (ex-info \"php.sp/remaining budget must be a non-negative integer\"\n    45\t                    {:yang.php.antlr.safepoint/error :invalid-budget\n    46\t                     :budget budget})))\n    47\t  (u/mark-tails\n    48\t    (u/then (definitions->uast function-definitions)\n    49\t            (u/then (u/def! 'php.sp/remaining (u/sexp->uast (list 'cell/new budget)))\n    50\t                    ast))))\ncollab/orch-lane/1794580000001-reviewer-retro-step7.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:9:**1. `6e587996` — P7-a budget: DEFECT**\ncollab/orch-lane/1794580000001-reviewer-retro-step7.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:11:- **P1 — Exhaustion can execute guest code and lose the fatal.** [safepoint.cljc:20](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/safepoint.cljc:20) calls `php/unwind-to`, which executes `:finally` thunks. Those include guest `finally` blocks and local-variable cleanup that can invoke destructors. A `finally { return 1; }` can escape before `php/raise` delivers the operational fatal. This violates §12’s non-catchable fatal and destructor-skipping contract. Forward fix: use a terminal root escape that guest handlers, returns, and finalizers cannot replace.\ncollab/orch-lane/1794580000001-reviewer-retro-step7.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:13:- **P1 — Exhaustion during shutdown takes the wrong result path.** [prelude.cljc:5092](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:5092) recognizes an operational failure only in `exc0`. A shutdown callback exhausting its budget produces operational `exc2`, which goes through `snapshot-exc` as though it were a guest Throwable. Forward fix: preserve operational termination throughout body, exception-handler, and shutdown execution.\ncollab/orch-lane/1794580000001-reviewer-retro-step7.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:21:- **P1 — `exit` uses exception unwinding instead of escaping directly to the task root.** [prelude.cljc:445](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:445) calls `php/raise`. Consequently, `try { exit(7); } finally { echo 'F'; }` executes `F`, and a return from `finally` can cancel termination. Exit during shutdown also lacks proper root handling. PHP executes shutdown functions and destructors after exit, but skips `finally` blocks. Forward fix: install a distinct root exit continuation and preserve its status through shutdown. [PHP exit semantics](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.exit.php)\ncollab/orch-lane/1794580000001-reviewer-retro-step7.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:23:- **P1 — Unclosed output buffers disappear.** [prelude.cljc:5082](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:5082) reads only buffer zero; `php/shutdown!` never drains the remaining buffers. Thus `ob_start(); echo 'x';` loses `x`. This directly violates row 22 and C22, including destructor output inside buffers. Forward fix: flush nested buffers after the destructor phase, before releasing globals.\ncollab/orch-lane/1793502000000-architect-p4-heap-ruling.kimi-k3.1-pro.stdout.log:5:{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"tool_FJEtGZkUxaGKp5G0m11jtFe9\",\"content\":\"1\\t# yang.php: the PHP frontend\\n2\\t\\n3\\tStatus: Designed 2026-10-17. Slices P1 and P2 (section 15) are implemented on\\n4\\tthe `yang.php.antlr` route (P2: arrays, key coercion, `foreach` by value, the\\n5\\tcore array builtins and sorting, oracle-verified against `php-8.3.35`, section\\n6\\t17.3). P3 landed in two parts: P3-a (the counted-cell substrate and the\\n7\\tprelude-level reference model, merged earlier) and P3-b (the program-level\\n8\\tlowering, see \\\"P3-b landing status\\\" below). P4 and P5 are complete; P6 is in\\n9\\tprogress through the linked inclusion profile, dynamic scope, namespaces,\\n10\\tconstants and autoload class map. P6's oracle recording and three-host DHT\\n11\\tverification remain part of its landing. The legacy `yang/php.cljc` frontend\\n12\\tis still the installed one. Design by Fable (Lead\\n13\\tSystem Architect seat); adversarial review by codex (gpt-6.1-sol) over a\\n14\\tnine-round design loop that ended READY with sign-off granted. Section 20\\n15\\t(\\\"Design provenance\\\") cites the loop.\\n16\\t\\n17\\t**P3-b landing status.** The program-level lowering of the P3 plan (steps S2 to\\n18\\tS12) lands on branch `yang-php-p3b`; the oracle is the pinned `php-8.3.35`\\n19\\t(`php:8.3-cli` on shiva, banner `php-8.3.35 shiva 2026-10-25`).\\n20\\t\\n21\\t- Lowered and oracle-pinned: references (`=&` in every location form,\\n22\\t  `[&$x]` array items), `global`, `static` binding to a persistent slot,\\n23\\t  by-reference parameters, variadics, named arguments and spread, dynamic\\n24\\t  calls, closures with `use` and `use (&)`, arrow functions, `foreach` by\\n25\\t  reference, the array-pointer builtins, destructuring (`[...]`, `list(...)`,\\n26\\t  keyed, nested, one level of `&`), `try`/`catch`/`finally`, `throw`, `match`,\\n27\\t  `Closure::bind` and `bindTo`, the temporary list, and the typed-reference\\n28\\t  machinery at prelude level (rows 41 to 45; syntax stays P4).\\n29\\t- Fixtures: 72 program-level fixtures are enabled and run on the AST walker,\\n30\\t  on the semantic, stack and register VMs, and parserless from checked-in CST\\n31\\t  packets on Node; 91 oracle results are pinned in `programs_p3b`\\n32\\t  (`recorded`), 13 further oracle-only sources record texts and the cyclic\\n33\\t  release evidence (R2 condition (a): `gc_collect_cycles()` collects the\\n34\\t  cycle, the engine only terminates, closed as a known gap in C1). The permutation\\n35\\t  corpus (13-letter alphabet, flat and nested) runs 518 programs in the quick\\n36\\t  tier and about 5,900 in the slow tier; the php-src `.phpt` selection tool is\\n37\\t  in place and its list is generated on shiva.\\n38\\t- Held: `:call-dynamic-dim` and `:foreach-key-and-destructure` (grammar corner\\n39\\t  C27); the user-class closure call `:closure-call-user-class` waits for P4.\\n40\\t- Not in this landing: the per-table iterator registry (C26, slice P3-c), the\\n41\\t  php-src selection, the full permutation corpus and the linked profile (P4\\n42\\t  seam).\\n43\\t\\n44\\t**Departures introduced by P3-b** are ratified as corners C24 to C29 (section\\n45\\t17.2) and recorded in `docs/agents/known-gaps.md`. `declare` is refused, so\\n46\\t`strict_types` for typed references has no program path; `ref-write!`'s\\n47\\t`strict` argument is the seam P4 wires when `declare` lowers.\\n48\\t\\n49\\tParent design: [`yang.antlr.md`](yang.antlr.md). This document makes concrete\\n50\\tits section 8.6 (Object-oriented and web: PHP, mixed text and ordered maps),\\n51\\tits section 8.11 for PHP arrays, and Phase 3. Companion:\\n52\\t[`yang.typescript.md`](yang.typescript.md), whose section 2 (prerequisites\\n53\\tbinding both frontends) and section 17 (the `yang.core` lifts) this document\\n54\\tdepends on and does not repeat. Once accepted, this document governs the\\n55\\tdetail; `yang.antlr.md` section 8.6 remains the paradigm summary.\\n56\\t\\n57\\tRequest records, bound surfaces and the refusal of ambient resolution follow\\n58\\tthe doctrine of ADR 0004 (\\\"external effects are interpreted stream deposits\\\"),\\n59\\twhich is Proposed and not yet committed; `yang.typescript.md` section 2.4\\n60\\tnames what its rejection would reopen.\\n61\\t\\n62\\t## 1. Scope\\n63\\t\\n64\\tA new ANTLR frontend replaces the current PHP compiler frontend (section 5).\\n65\\tIt compiles PHP 8.3 source, including\\n66\\tmixed HTML text, to the Universal AST, and runs one request as one task\\n67\\treading an explicit request record. The frontend id is `:yang/php` either\\n68\\tway — the one-frontend-per-language scheme (2026-10-18; the id was\\n69\\t`:yang.php/legacy` before the legacy suffix was retired) leaves no room\\n70\\tfor implementation-suffixed ids, so the ANTLR frontend lands as a new\\n71\\trevision of `:yang/php`, its implementation named by the manifest's\\n72\\trevision and profile keys; the final naming at acceptance is the owner's.\\n73\\t\\n74\\tThe design claims a specified fragment, not Zend fidelity: the transition\\n75\\ttable of section 4 and the behaviour of the slices of section 15, each pinned\\n76\\tby oracle fixtures against the pinned PHP CLI, with every Zend behaviour\\n77\\toutside it listed as a named corner (section 17). The `yang.antlr.md` Phase 3\\n78\\texit criteria are direct fixtures:\\n79\\t\\n80\\t1. mixed text and code produce output in statement order;\\n81\\t2. arrays keep insertion order and PHP's alias and copy semantics;\\n82\\t3. superglobals are built from an explicit request input;\\n83\\t4. type declarations are not silently ignored (including through references);\\n84\\t5. two requests share nothing.\\n85\\t\\n86\\tLike every frontend: no new AST tag, no engine change, no codec change.\\n87\\t\\n88\\t## 2. Prerequisites\\n89\\t\\n90\\tEverything in `yang.typescript.md` section 2 binds this frontend: defects D1\\n91\\tto D7, the shared rulings (own null sentinel, Jing numeric kinds, JVM-only\\n92\\tparsing with three-host execution, the linked-profile matrix of bundled on\\n93\\tfour evaluators and linked on three until L-b), and the ADR 0004 dependency.\\n94\\tPHP uses the lifted namespaces of `yang.typescript.md` section 17 (L1 to L4\\n95\\tland first) and adds nothing to them (section 16). Two PHP-specific\\n96\\tdependencies: statement-level ticks wait on safepoint slice 3 (section 12),\\n97\\tand optional absent includes wait on the known-gaps ledger item for\\n98\\tpublication-time-unavailable imports (section 11.3).\\n99\\t\\n100\\t## 3. The paradigm specification\\n101\\t\\n102\\t### 3.1 Reference runtime\\n103\\t\\n104\\tPHP 8.3 CLI at a pinned patch release (owner decision B8.1), with `php.ini`\\n105\\tpinning `precision=14`, `serialize_precision=-1`, `error_reporting=E_ALL` and\\n106\\t`display_errors=stderr`. The grammar's feature set (enums, `match`,\\n107\\t`readonly`, named arguments, attributes) is PHP 8.1 and later, so 8.3 is the\\n108\\tnatural pin. PHP 8.4 features (property hooks, asymmetric visibility) are not\\n109\\tparsed (corner C10).\\n110\\t\\n111\\t### 3.2 Arrays and keys\\n112\\t\\n113\\tA PHP array is a value: assignment and parameter passing copy it, and a write\\n114\\tnever affects another holder. Its representation is chosen by what it contains\\n115\\t(section 4.2): an array that transitively holds no reference, no\\n116\\tarray-instance handle and no object handle is a **plain** persistent ordered\\n117\\tmap stored directly in its holder, copied in O(1), with mutation as get, pure\\n118\\tupdate and `cell/set!`. Any other array is a counted **instance** cell whose\\n119\\tduplication follows the engine (section 4).\\n120\\t\\n121\\t- **Key coercion** at every write and read: an integer stays; a\\n122\\t  decimal-integer string in canonical form (`\\\"8\\\"` yes; `\\\"08\\\"`, `\\\"8.0\\\"`, `\\\" 8\\\"`\\n123\\t  no) becomes that integer; a float truncates toward zero (with the PHP 8.1\\n124\\t  deprecation emitted as a diagnostic record); `true` and `false` become 1 and\\n125\\t  0; `null` becomes `\\\"\\\"`; any other string stays a string.\\n126\\t- `next-index` is one more than the largest integer key ever used and never\\n127\\t  decreases on unset; negative keys follow PHP 8.3's rule (next is max + 1).\\n128\\t  `$a[] = v` appends at `next-index`. The one decrement is `array_pop`, which\\n129\\t  lowers it when the popped key was `next-index - 1`.\\n130\\t- Iteration follows the key order (`php/array-keys`; the prelude's array\\n131\\t  operations are the only code that reads `:index`, `:order` or `:next-index`).\\n132\\t  Unset splices the order. Nested arrays are nested\\n133\\t  values: `$a['x']['y'] = 1` is one path update.\\n134\\t- Large arrays cost one path copy per write; a packed-vector fast path is an\\n135\\t  attached optimizer's business, not the lowering's.\\n136\\t\\n137\\t#### 3.2.1 Reads, `isset`, `empty`, `unset` and builtins (P2)\\n138\\t\\n139\\t- **Reads.** A missing key warns (2) `Undefined array key \\\"x\\\"` or\\n140\\t  `Undefined array key 5` and answers null; an offset read on null, bool, int\\n141\\t  or float warns `Trying to access array offset on <type|true|false>`; a\\n142\\t  string container answers the one byte at the offset (negative counts from the\\n143\\t  end), out of range warns `Uninitialized string offset N` and answers `\\\"\\\"`.\\n144\\t  `$a[]` in read position is a compile error `Cannot use [] for reading`.\\n145\\t- **`isset`/`empty` on a string container** coerce the offset, then test the\\n146\\t  range: int itself; `true`, `false`, null as `1`, `0`, `0` silently; a float\\n147\\t  by the float-key seam (`php/float-key`, precision deprecation when\\n148\\t  fractional, no \\\"String offset cast\\\" warning, which belongs to reads); an\\n149\\t  integer-numeric string as that int; anything else is not an offset (`isset`\\n150\\t  false, `empty` true).\\n151\\t- **`unset` on a non-array**: a missing key or null at any level is a no-op;\\n152\\t  a string terminal is `Error \\\"Cannot unset string offsets\\\"`; int, float and\\n153\\t  `true` are `Error \\\"Cannot unset offset in a non-array variable\\\"`; `false`\\n154\\t  emits Deprecated (8192) `Automatic conversion of false to array is\\n155\\t  deprecated` and stays `false`.\\n156\\t- **Builtin protocol.** One table (`yang.php.antlr.builtins/supported`) feeds\\n157\\t  the prelude registration and the lowering's by-reference positions. For a\\n158\\t  call with `:by-ref` positions the lowering (1) binds each by-ref location's\\n159\\t  keys at that argument's place in left-to-right order; (2) evaluates every\\n160\\t  other argument left to right into temporaries (a non-location at a by-ref\\n161\\t  position warns 8 `Only variables should be passed by reference`); (3)\\n162\\t  immediately before the call, reads each by-ref location through its bound\\n163\\t  keys and builds the argument vector; (4) calls; (5) writes each out back\\n164\\t  through the same keys, in position order.\\n165\\t- **`foreach` by value** iterates a snapshot (`php/foreach-array`, then\\n166\\t  `php/array-keys` and `php/array-get`), so writes, `unset`, appends and\\n167\\t  `sort` of the source in the body never change the iteration (the by-value\\n168\\t  half of C15). A non-array warns and iterates nothing.\\n169\\t- **Sorting** is one stable merge sort in the prelude (no host sort), with\\n170\\t  flags `SORT_REGULAR`, `SORT_NUMERIC`, `SORT_STRING`; `r*` variants flip the\\n171\\t  sign without reversing equal elements. A **bool** comparator is retried\\n172\\t  swapped and emits Deprecated 8192 `usort(): Returning bool from comparison\\n173\\t  function is deprecated, return an integer less than, equal to, or greater\\n174\\t  than zero` once per call. Parity claim: `usort` with a bool comparator\\n175\\t  `return $a > $b;` over `[3, 1, 2]` matches the CLI's order and emits the\\n176\\t  deprecation exactly once per call. Stability is not parity for a comparator\\n177\\t  that disagrees with itself (corner G-sort-inconsistent-cmp, section 17.2).\\n178\\t\\n179\\t### 3.3 Scope\\n180\\t\\n181\\t- PHP has function scope only, with no block scope and no implicit closure\\n182\\t  capture, except arrow `fn`, which captures by value every enclosing variable\\n183\\t  it mentions, at creation.\\n184\\t- **Locals are cells** allocated at function entry with `:php/undef`; reading\\n185\\t  one emits `Warning: Undefined variable` and yields null. Every function body\\n186\\t  that declares locals or parameters releases them on exit (row 16).\\n187\\t- `global $x` binds the local to the `$GLOBALS` entry by reference (row 17),\\n188\\t  creating it as null if absent.\\n189\\t- `static $x = <expr>;` is a cell owned by the function object, allocated at\\n190\\t  function creation with an uninitialized sentinel. Its initializer is an\\n191\\t  arbitrary expression evaluated **when execution first reaches the\\n192\\t  declaration** (PHP 8.3) and filled once.\\n193\\t- **The dynamic-scope profile**: a function whose body contains\\n194\\t  `include`/`require`, `$$name`, `extract`, `compact`, `get_defined_vars`,\\n195\\t  `eval`, or one-argument `parse_str` keeps its locals in a heap symbol-table\\n196\\t  dict (the precedent is Python's module namespace, owner decision 1 of\\n197\\t  `yang.antlr.md` 8.5.1). Scope analysis decides per function, and the\\n198\\t  lowering targets `php/local-get`/`php/local-set` on the dict. All other\\n199\\t  functions use cells (owner decision B8.3). The symbol table holds reference\\n200\\t  markers like any location.\\n201\\t- **The global scope** is always the `$GLOBALS` dict: a heap dict,\\n202\\t  insertion-ordered, with the superglobals pre-seeded from the request record.\\n203\\t  `$GLOBALS` is read-only as of PHP 8.1 except through its elements (corner\\n204\\t  C13).\\n205\\t\\n206\\t### 3.4 Superglobals and the request context\\n207\\t\\n208\\t- A PHP run is one task consuming one **request record** from a\\n209\\t  composition-supplied stream: `{:php.request/method :uri :query :headers\\n210\\t  :cookies :body :server :env :files}`. `php/init!` builds `$_GET`, `$_POST`,\\n211\\t  `$_COOKIE`, `$_SERVER`, `$_ENV`, `$_FILES`, `$_REQUEST` (ordered per a\\n212\\t  pinned `request_order`) and, for CLI, `$argv` and `$argc`. `$_SESSION` is a\\n213\\t  bound surface (a session-store effect), absent by default. Nothing reads\\n214\\t  ambient process or HTTP state.\\n215\\t- **Output** (`echo`, `print`, inline HTML, `printf`) appends to an output\\n216\\t  cell with the output-buffering stack; `ob_start` and `ob_get_clean` are pure\\n217\\t  prelude over that stack. The top level flushes ordered `:php.response/body`\\n218\\t  records on the response stream. `header()`, `http_response_code` and\\n219\\t  `setcookie` produce `:php.response/header` records, refused after the first\\n220\\t  body flush with PHP's warning text.\\n221\\t- `exit` and `die` are an escape to the task root, followed by the shutdown\\n222\\t  sequence (row 22).\\n223\\t- Cross-request leakage is impossible by construction: one task per request\\n224\\t  and no store writes for guest state.\\n225\\t\\n226\\t### 3.5 Coercion, comparison and arithmetic\\n227\\t\\n228\\t- **Loose equality** is the PHP 8 comparison table, pinned as a fixture matrix\\n229\\t  over every pair of `null`, `true`, `false`, `0`, `1`, `-1`, `\\\"0\\\"`, `\\\"\\\"`,\\n230\\t  `\\\"1\\\"`, `\\\"01\\\"`, `\\\"1e1\\\"`, `\\\"10\\\"`, `\\\"abc\\\"`, `\\\" 1\\\"`, `\\\"1 \\\"`, `[]`, `[0]`, `0.0`,\\n231\\t  `NAN`, `INF`, an object, and a `Stringable`. For example `0 == \\\"\\\"`, `0 ==\\n232\\t  []` and `\\\"\\\" == []` are false in PHP 8, while `0 == \\\"0\\\"`, `\\\"\\\" == null`, `[]\\n233\\t  == false`, `\\\"1\\\" == \\\"01\\\"`, `\\\"10\\\" == \\\"1e1\\\"` and `100 == \\\"1e2\\\"` are true.\\n234\\t  Number versus numeric string compares numerically; number versus non-numeric\\n235\\t  string compares as strings, so `0 == \\\"a\\\"` is false. Arrays compare by key\\n236\\t  and value; objects by property, or by identity for `===`.\\n237\\t- `<=>` uses the same ordering; `===` requires identical type and value,\\n238\\t  arrays element-wise, objects the same handle.\\n239\\t- **Numeric strings** (`is_numeric`) allow leading and trailing whitespace\\n240\\t  (8.0) and exponent forms. Leading-numeric strings in arithmetic warn (`\\\"5\\n241\\t  apples\\\" + 1` warns and gives 6); non-numeric ones throw `TypeError`. String\\n242\\t  increment is supported (`\\\"a\\\"++` is `\\\"b\\\"`, `\\\"Az\\\"++` is `\\\"Ba\\\"`).\\n243\\t- **Integer overflow converts to float**: every integer operator goes through\\n244\\t  the exact integer module and demotes to float64 when the exact result leaves\\n245\\t  `[-2^63, 2^63-1]` (`PHP_INT_MAX + 1` is a float). This is the one place C3's\\n246\\t  promotion-before-overflow is used to detect rather than to promote.\\n247\\t- `/` yields an int only when exact, else a float. `%` is int-only:\\n248\\t  `PHP_INT_MIN % -1` is `0`, and `% 0` throws `DivisionByZeroError`.\\n249\\t  `intdiv(PHP_INT_MIN, -1)` throws `ArithmeticError`, and `intdiv(x, 0)`\\n250\\t  throws `DivisionByZeroError`. `fmod` never throws. `**` is an int when exact\\n251\\t  and in range, else a float, via the pinned `fpow`.\\n252\\t- **Float to string**: `echo` uses `precision=14` (`%.14G`, so `echo 0.1+0.2`\\n253\\t  prints `0.3`); `var_dump`, `var_export` and `json_encode` use\\n254\\t  `serialize_precision=-1` (the shortest round trip, `integer/float-digits`).\\n255\\t  Fourteen-significant-digit rounding needs exact decimal rounding of a\\n256\\t  binary64: a prelude function over `integer`'s exact decomposition, with no\\n257\\t  new kernel (an `integer` version bump only if measured too slow).\\n258\\t\\n259\\t### 3.6 Null\\n260\\t\"}\ncollab/orch-lane/1793502000000-architect-p4-heap-ruling.kimi-k3.1-pro.stdout.log:6:{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"tool_NDIHy8XoOEofY0uedKrQMYRa\",\"content\":\"1\\t(ns yang.php.antlr.generators-test\\n2\\t  \\\"P4 S8 generators, live iteration and protocol integration on the JVM.\\\"\\n3\\t  (:require\\n4\\t    [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\\n5\\t    [yang.core.uast :as u]\\n6\\t    [yang.php.antlr.e2e-harness :as h]\\n7\\t    [yang.php.antlr.programs-p4 :as p4]\\n8\\t    [yang.tails :as tails]\\n9\\t    [yin.vm :as vm]\\n10\\t    [yin.vm.debruijn.register :as rvm]\\n11\\t    [yin.vm.debruijn.stack :as dvm]\\n12\\t    [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\\n13\\t    [yin.vm.semantic :as semantic]\\n14\\t    [yin.vm.semantic.derive :as derive]\\n15\\t    [yin.vm.semantic.linearize :as sr-linearize]\\n16\\t    [yin.vm.test-utils :as tu]\\n17\\t    [yin.vm.values :as values])\\n18\\t  (:import\\n19\\t    [java.util IdentityHashMap]))\\n20\\t\\n21\\t\\n22\\t(def ^:private vms [:ast-walker :semantic :stack :register])\\n23\\t\\n24\\t\\n25\\t(deftest generator-fixtures-on-every-vm-test\\n26\\t  (doseq [{:keys [id source out]} p4/generators-v1]\\n27\\t    (testing (name id)\\n28\\t      (let [results (h/run-php source)]\\n29\\t        (is (not (contains? results :diagnostics)) (pr-str results))\\n30\\t        (doseq [k vms]\\n31\\t          (is (= out (get results k)) (str (name id) \\\" on \\\" k)))))))\\n32\\t\\n33\\t\\n34\\t(deftest iteration-protocols-test\\n35\\t  (testing \\\"Iterator drives foreach directly\\\"\\n36\\t    (is (= \\\"01\\\" (:php/out (h/run-raw\\n37\\t                            \\\"<?php class I implements Iterator { private $i=0; function rewind(): void {$this->i=0;} function valid(): bool {return $this->i<2;} function current(): mixed {return $this->i;} function key(): mixed {return $this->i;} function next(): void {$this->i++;} } foreach(new I as $v) echo $v;\\\")))))\\n38\\t  (testing \\\"IteratorAggregate supplies the live iterator\\\"\\n39\\t    (is (= \\\"23\\\" (:php/out (h/run-raw\\n40\\t                            \\\"<?php class I implements Iterator {private $i=2;function rewind():void{}function valid():bool{return $this->i<4;}function current(){return $this->i;}function key(){return $this->i;}function next():void{$this->i++;}} class A implements IteratorAggregate {function getIterator():Iterator{return new I;}} foreach(new A as $v)echo $v;\\\")))))\\n41\\t  (testing \\\"property iteration filters visibility from the calling scope\\\"\\n42\\t    (is (= \\\"aabc\\\" (:php/out (h/run-raw\\n43\\t                              \\\"<?php class P {public $a='a'; protected $b='b'; private $c='c'; function f(){foreach($this as $v)echo $v;}} $p=new P;foreach($p as $v)echo $v;$p->f();\\\")))))\\n44\\t  (testing \\\"ArrayAccess reads by value; Countable and Stringable dispatch\\\"\\n45\\t    (is (= \\\"43s\\\" (:php/out (h/run-raw\\n46\\t                             \\\"<?php class A implements ArrayAccess, Countable, Stringable {function offsetExists($x):bool{return true;} function offsetGet($x){return 4;} function offsetSet($x,$v):void{} function offsetUnset($x):void{} function count():int{return 3;} function __toString():string{return 's';}} $a=new A;echo $a[0],count($a),(string)$a;\\\"))))))\\n47\\t\\n48\\t\\n49\\t(def ^:private opts\\n50\\t  {:make-stream tu/make-stream\\n51\\t   :capability-secret tu/secret\\n52\\t   :primitives vm/primitives\\n53\\t   :modules (h/host-registry)})\\n54\\t\\n55\\t\\n56\\t(def ^:private load-semantic\\n57\\t  (vm/fresh-code-loader semantic/load-ast vm/ast-contract))\\n58\\t\\n59\\t\\n60\\t(def ^:private final-vms\\n61\\t  {:ast-walker (fn [ast _] (vm/eval (tu/create-vm opts) ast))\\n62\\t   :semantic (fn [_ datoms] (vm/run (load-semantic (semantic/create-vm opts) datoms)))\\n63\\t   :stack (fn [ast _]\\n64\\t            (vm/run (dvm/create-vm\\n65\\t                      (:image (derive/stack-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast))))\\n66\\t                      (assoc opts :contract vm/stack-contract))))\\n67\\t   :register (fn [ast _]\\n68\\t               (vm/run (rvm/create-vm\\n69\\t                         (derive/register-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast)))\\n70\\t                         (assoc opts :contract vm/register-contract))))})\\n71\\t\\n72\\t\\n73\\t(defn- deep-size\\n74\\t  \\\"Count shared compound values once, including closure/continuation payloads.\\\"\\n75\\t  [x]\\n76\\t  (let [seen (IdentityHashMap.)]\\n77\\t    (loop [work [x], size 0]\\n78\\t      (if (empty? work)\\n79\\t        size\\n80\\t        (let [x (peek work)\\n81\\t              work (pop work)\\n82\\t              compound? (or (values/host-typed? x) (coll? x))]\\n83\\t          (if (and compound? (.put seen x true))\\n84\\t            (recur work size)\\n85\\t            (recur (cond\\n86\\t                     (values/host-typed? x) (conj work (values/payload x))\\n87\\t                     (map? x) (into (into work (keys x)) (vals x))\\n88\\t                     (coll? x) (into work x)\\n89\\t                     :else work)\\n90\\t                   (inc size))))))))\\n91\\t\\n92\\t\\n93\\t(defn- live-after-collection\\n94\\t  [k source]\\n95\\t  (let [lowered (:ast (h/lower-program source))\\n96\\t        ;; Keep the normal unit entry point, but block its body before it\\n97\\t        ;; returns to php/run-main and shutdown. This fresh stream has no\\n98\\t        ;; producer, so h stays live and suspended in the captured heap.\\n99\\t        blocker {:type :stream/next\\n100\\t                 :source {:type :stream/cursor\\n101\\t                          :source {:type :stream/make :buffer 1}}}\\n102\\t        ast (tails/remark-tails\\n103\\t              (update-in lowered [:operator :body :operands 0 :body]\\n104\\t                         u/then blocker))\\n105\\t        blocked ((get final-vms k) ast (vm/ast->datoms ast))]\\n106\\t    (when (is (vm/blocked? blocked) (str k \\\" blocked before shutdown\\\"))\\n107\\t      (let [roots (into []\\n108\\t                        (keep (fn [[id {:keys [value seal]}]]\\n109\\t                                (when (and (= \\\"h\\\" (get-in value [:gen :name]))\\n110\\t                                           (= :suspended (get-in value [:gen :state])))\\n111\\t                                  {:type :cell-ref :id id :seal seal})))\\n112\\t                        (:heap blocked))]\\n113\\t        (when (is (= 1 (count roots)) (str k \\\" one suspended delegating generator\\\"))\\n114\\t          (let [heap (:heap (engine/collect blocked roots))]\\n115\\t            [(count heap)\\n116\\t             (deep-size (mapv :value (vals heap)))]))))))\\n117\\t\\n118\\t\\n119\\t(defn- changing-caller-source\\n120\\t  [n]\\n121\\t  (str \\\"<?php function g() { $i=0; while (true) { yield $i; $i++; } }\\\"\\n122\\t       ;; Own the delegate across caller statements, whose temporary cleanup\\n123\\t       ;; would otherwise release the bare g() result while h is suspended.\\n124\\t       \\\" function h() { $d=g(); yield from $d; } $it=h(); $it->self=$it;\\\"\\n125\\t       \\\" function byFunction($x) { $x->next(); }\\\"\\n126\\t       \\\" function byGenerator($x) { yield $x->current(); $x->next(); }\\\"\\n127\\t       \\\" $k=0; while ($k < \\\" n \\\") {\\\"\\n128\\t       \\\" if ($k % 2 == 0) { byFunction($it); }\\\"\\n129\\t       \\\" else { $j=byGenerator($it); $j->current(); $j->next(); } $k++; }\\\"))\\n130\\t\\n131\\t\\n132\\t(deftest ^:slow reachable-heap-is-stable-under-delegation-test\\n133\\t  (doseq [k vms]\\n134\\t    (testing (str k)\\n135\\t      (let [small (live-after-collection k (changing-caller-source 10))\\n136\\t            large (live-after-collection k (changing-caller-source 1000))]\\n137\\t        (when (and small large)\\n138\\t          (is (= small large)))))))\"}\ncollab/orch-lane/1793502000000-architect-p4-heap-ruling.kimi-k3.1-pro.stdout.log:7:{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"tool_Ea8ix43e4V4o7klpV2NKTdHV\",\"content\":\"docs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:29:explicit release. Retaining timed-out requests leaked payloads for nothing;\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:34:§5.2 #6 asserted a connect-timeout cleanup the transport does not perform.\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:247:| S2 | `stop!` detaches every session, releases the listener, stops the ticker; idempotent | `[D]` **new** |\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:333:`jing-coordinate/open!` failing before a store is retained (its P5 cleanup\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:668:   completing it releases the second in submission order.\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:858:3. `request-timeout-retires-the-call-and-the-client-recovers-once-released`\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:887:   because that is all the transport guarantees (N2)**: no cleanup claim, no\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:898:   handle)` is true, a second `jing/close!` is a no-op; release the latch.\\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:100:**The lineage is leased** (owner ruling 2026-10-07; the holder choice below is the architect's). A lineage nobody returns to is reclaimed through `dao.lease.md`, not by a timer. The peer holding the parked record is the grantor. When a lineage begins, its judge grants unsolicited on the lease medium: subject and holder `{:dao.star/lineage S}`, duration the composition's idle allowance, `:dao.lease/max` optional. Attribution is by envelope key: the session-facts medium is written only by session drivers, each stamping `:dao.star/lineage` on its facts, renewals and release, and the judge's resolver reads that key; until ShiBi, any writer of that medium can renew, the same exposure the exchange lease has. The driver observes its grant before consuming or projecting, renews at under half the duration from its own control flow, on each consumed input and on its tick while it holds an open subscription, and **stops at its bound**: past it, it consumes nothing and projects nothing, and an input arriving then is handed to the root program as begin. So a connected session is retained for as long as it stays connected, by design, bounded only by the cap; a disconnected one lapses after the idle allowance. The lease ends with the lineage: released by the driver on halt, lapsed by the judge on silence. Reclaim retires the runnable resource, the current parked record; the `:lapsed` record on the lease medium is the judge's own and is what ends the lineage for the router. History, successor facts and lease records are kept (`dao.lease.md`: a reclaim frees the resource, never the record). The subject is distinct from Mode 2's custody subject `{:yin.k/occurrence O}` on the arbitration medium. Leases are process-scoped until the composition settles the three durable duties `dao.lease.md` names.\\ndocs/design/dao.agent.harness.md:204:that the seat releases the claim (`:claim/status :released`) and the\\ndocs/design/dao.agent.har